WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.420
Meta’s Twitter copycat isn’t&nbsp;
quite living up to expectations.

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:08.460
Days after launching on July 5th,&nbsp;
engagement has dropped off a cliff.

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:13.380
Time spent on the app declined 60%&nbsp;
from its peak a few days prior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.660
according to the software company SensorTower.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.180
And this was during the first week it launched.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:22.800
Similarly, software company SimilarWeb&nbsp;
reported Threads’ daily active users&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.640
dropped from 49 million to 24 million in a week.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.400
It’s even more disappointing considering Threads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.100
had the greatest start of&nbsp;
any social media app ever.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:38.220
It reached 100 million users in five days -&nbsp;
surpassing even the rapid rise of ChatGPT.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:40.440
So, what went wrong?

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.500
While the initial interest&nbsp;
may have appeared promising,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.900
it’s possible this was somewhat misleading.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:50.040
One factor contributing to the surge&nbsp;
of sign-ups how easy it is to join.

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:52.680
I could easily get my puppy onto Threads by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:55.980
signing up through her Instagram&nbsp;
account with just a few clicks.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:58.500
Additionally, Threads offers the feature of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:01.800
transferring your Instagram followers&nbsp;
directly onto the new platform.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.620
This could have amplified&nbsp;
the popularity of Threads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.740
giving a skewed perception&nbsp;
of genuine user engagement.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:11.520
Another aspect that’s problematic&nbsp;
is that you can’t delete your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:14.460
Threads account without deleting&nbsp;
your Instagram account as well.

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:19.260
You can deactivate Threads, but you’re&nbsp;
locked in unless you choose to delete both.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:22.020
And privacy concerns are off the charts.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.680
When I compare the privacy labels from&nbsp;
Apple's App Store, I discovered that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:31.020
Threads gathers more information than&nbsp;
Twitter, including “sensitive info”.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:36.540
That includes racial or ethnic data, sexual&nbsp;
orientation, pregnancy or childbirth information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:42.660
disability, religious and philosophical beliefs,&nbsp;
trade union membership, political opinion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:49.020
genetic information, or biometric data - which&nbsp;
includes fingerprints and facial recognition data.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:53.640
Because it collects so much data, Threads&nbsp;
isn’t yet able to launch in Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:58.920
as collecting sensitive information requires&nbsp;
a higher standard of legal consent in the EU.&nbsp;

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.040
Meta’s track record on privacy is far from clean.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:06.780
Facebook is known for collecting user data&nbsp;
and allowing advertisers to target them.

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:10.860
Besides privacy concerns,&nbsp;
Threads has limited features.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:14.700
Unlike Twitter, there’s no option of a&nbsp;
home feed just for people you follow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:17.220
though such a feature is in the works.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:21.060
Instead, the Threads algorithm&nbsp;
pushes content from people you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:23.940
don’t follow based on what it&nbsp;
determines to be most engaging.

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:26.640
The goal is to keep you on&nbsp;
the platform for longer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.940
exposing you to more ads when ads come to Threads.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:33.420
Additionally, the inability to&nbsp;
view posts chronologically on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:37.500
the timeline or search for specific&nbsp;
posts restricts the user experience.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.280
The true success of imitators&nbsp;
typically comes from offering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.320
an improved version of the original product.

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:48.120
Like when Instagram took inspiration from&nbsp;
Snapchat to create Instagram Stories.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:51.720
Instagram added new features, such&nbsp;
as the option to pause, rewind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:56.220
and fast-forward through stories, something&nbsp;
that wasn’t available on Snapchat at the time.

00:02:56.220 --> 00:03:01.740
This enhanced the user experience, and Instagram&nbsp;
Stories surpassed Snapchat’s user base.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:05.160
Threads, however, doesn’t&nbsp;
really improve upon Twitter.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:08.340
It mirrors Twitter - it’s hard&nbsp;
to tell them apart - other than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:10.200
for a few missing features mentioned earlier.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:13.980
Meta’s sales pitch for Threads&nbsp;
appears to be that it’s not Twitter.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.000
Zuckerberg has been trying to&nbsp;
brand Threads as a “kind” and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.080
“friendly” place where politics and&nbsp;
news apparently won’t be emphasized.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:26.340
However, it's the back-and-forth on&nbsp;
politics, news, and controversial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:30.960
topics that makes Twitter so compelling,&nbsp;
drawing people in and keeping them engaged.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:35.160
By the way, I’d be grateful if you followed&nbsp;
@Newsthink on Twitter. Link in description.

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:41.460
Tucker Carlson’s interview with Andrew Tate&nbsp;
has been seen by 92 million users on Twitter.

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:46.380
Tate is a controversial figure who was banned&nbsp;
from the platform prior to Musk taking over.

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:51.540
A big reason the SpaceX and Tesla CEO&nbsp;
acquired Twitter for $44 billion was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:54.060
because he felt freedom of&nbsp;
speech had been restricted.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:56.880
Twitter has been bracing for more competition.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.260
About a week after Threads launched, Twitter&nbsp;
started sharing ad revenue with creators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:04.680
beginning with a portion of the revenue&nbsp;
earned from replies to their posts.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:09.540
So Twitter is trying to incentivize people to&nbsp;
post great content there, bolstering its appeal.

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:11.820
But maybe it doesn’t have to&nbsp;
worry about the competition.

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:16.800
Will Threads end up in the graveyard of&nbsp;
other failed Meta projects, like Lasso,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:20.460
Facebook’s answer to TikTok, or&nbsp;
Hobbi, its answer to Pinterest?

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:24.840
The huge drop in engagement so soon&nbsp;
after launch suggests it’s headed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:27.240
down that direction unless the app improves.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:31.080
But maybe…this wasn’t about&nbsp;
Threads but a bigger plan.

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:37.260
Threads could be part of Zuckerberg’s wider&nbsp;
goal to build the metaverse, a virtual reality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:41.880
space that enables users to interact with each&nbsp;
other inside a computer-generated environment.

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:47.520
Meta says it plans to decentralize Threads,&nbsp;
meaning it will work with other non-Meta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:52.380
social networks, which aligns with Meta’s vision&nbsp;
for the metaverse, a seamless online ecosystem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:56.700
where interactions are not limited to&nbsp;
one specific platform like Facebook.

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:00.840
The rivalry with Twitter is not&nbsp;
confined to the virtual world.

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:05.520
Elon Musk and Mark Zuckerberg may be&nbsp;
stepping into an entirely different ring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:08.220
possibly fighting in an MMA-style match!

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:11.580
Who would you place your bet&nbsp;
on? Let me know in the comments.

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:13.860
As fascinating as this fight would be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:18.840
I really do hope they’re not seriously injured&nbsp;
as their tech companies need their big brains.

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:22.200
The brains behind their&nbsp;
platforms are skilled engineers.

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:25.800
It’s the kind of skill that is so&nbsp;
important to foster - especially today.

